# Default object permissions for the PolySwarm App.
#
# WHY THE EXPORT SCOPE IS SPLIT (ruled 2026-09-09). Splunk's default.meta
# specification is the authority for both halves of this. An object is visible
# only inside the app that ships it until its export scope says system, and an
# object exported to system gets no change to its accessibility rules: a
# reader still needs read access to the containing app, to the object class,
# and to the object itself. Export scope and role access are therefore two
# independent axes, and this file uses both. Exported: the things a search
# running in some other app has to be able to resolve, which is the five
# search commands and their search-assistant syntax, the three workflow
# actions, the alert action, the lookup definitions, the KV store collections
# those definitions read, and the props, eventtypes and tags that make the
# intel sourcetype searchable and CIM compliant. Not exported: credentials,
# configuration and user interface, which is the passwords classes, the app
# object, polyswarm.conf, restmap, the modular inputs, the views and the nav.
# Nothing outside this app needs to read those, and the credential path
# especially must not be reachable from a namespace nobody reviewed. Every
# access line below is the one it had before the split, so least privilege is
# unaffected: it lives in the role lists, not in the export scope.
#
# WHY sc_admin APPEARS BESIDE admin IN EVERY LIST (ruled 2026-09-09). sc_admin
# is the Splunk Cloud Platform customer's own administrator role: Splunk's
# Enterprise Security installation manual, in "Users and roles for Splunk
# Enterprise Security" on help.splunk.com, states that "In a Splunk Cloud
# Platform deployment, the Splunk platform admin role is called sc_admin", and
# the Splunk Cloud Platform Service Details page states that "Splunk Cloud
# Platform provides the sc_admin role, which has sufficient capabilities to
# administer Splunk Cloud Platform". The admin role itself is not handed to a
# Cloud customer, so an object whose only named role was admin would be an
# object that customer's administrator could not reach, which is what
# AppInspect's check_kos_are_accessible warns about. Adding the role costs
# nothing on Splunk Enterprise: the default.meta.conf specification describes
# access as a list of named roles, and a reader is granted the object when a
# role they hold appears in that list at the app, class and object level, so a
# name nobody on the instance holds matches nobody and grants nothing. Splunk
# Enterprise ships no sc_admin role, and no Splunk page found says in so many
# words what splunkd does with a role name it cannot resolve, so that half is
# read off the specification's own matching model rather than quoted; what is
# quoted is that role lists are matched by name. The stanza comments below that
# read "admin and power only" describe the same gate they always did: sc_admin
# travels with admin everywhere in this file and nowhere else, so it widens no
# gate and it withholds nothing from power or user that was reaching them.
#
# WHY polyswarm_analyst APPEARS BESIDE power (ruled 2026-09-09, decision D27).
# An object ACL is not the only gate on the credential path. splunkd protects
# its storage/passwords endpoint with a capability as well, and Splunk's
# capability reference, "Define roles on the Splunk platform with
# capabilities" on help.splunk.com, says list_storage_passwords is what "Lets
# the user list and view the /storage/passwords endpoint, lets the user perform
# GETs". The Power column of that table is not marked for that row, and the
# built-in power role does not hold the capability. Every metered command in
# this app reads the key from that endpoint as the searching user, so power
# passed every ACL below and was still refused the key: confirmed live on
# Splunk Enterprise 10.4.2, HTTP 403 on the app's storage/passwords path and a
# ClientConfigurationError from every metered command.
#
# default/authorize.conf therefore ships one custom role, polyswarm_analyst,
# which imports power and enables that one capability. This file names it
# wherever it names power, in read and write clauses alike, and adds it to the
# read clause of [passwords] and [storage/passwords] so that the object gate
# and the capability gate line up on the same role. power keeps every grant it
# had, which costs nothing (a role that cannot read the credential cannot use
# the commands whatever this file says) and leaves the other route open: an
# administrator who would rather add list_storage_passwords to power than
# assign the shipped role can, and then power is the role that holds both
# halves. Built-in roles are not modified anywhere in this app.
#
# The stanza comments below that read "admin and power only" describe the same
# gate they always did, in the same way they already do for sc_admin:
# polyswarm_analyst travels with power everywhere in this file, so it widens no
# gate and it withholds nothing. Where a comment says a gate excludes the user
# role, it still does.
#
# Five rules govern this file:
#
#   1. Export scope is a decision per object, and it is one of two values. A
#      stanza a search in another app must resolve sets export = system; a
#      stanza carrying a credential, a piece of configuration or a view sets
#      export = none. Adding a stanza means classifying it, and the tests
#      enumerate both lists so that a new one cannot arrive unclassified.
#   2. Every object class is listed explicitly. There is no reliance on the
#      root stanza to cover an object type by omission, because a default that
#      is inherited silently is a default nobody reviews.
#   3. No read : [ * ] on anything in the credential path. Roles are named.
#      A wildcard read on a credential object grants every present and future
#      role, including ones created after this file was last looked at.
#   4. Every access list that names admin also names sc_admin, in both the
#      read clause and the write clause, so that a Splunk Cloud customer's
#      administrator reaches whatever this app's own administrator reaches. A
#      test asserts this over the whole file rather than stanza by stanza, so
#      a stanza added later cannot forget it.
#   5. Every access list that names power also names polyswarm_analyst, in the
#      write clause as well as the read clause, so the role holding the
#      credential capability reaches everything power reaches. Swept over the
#      whole file for the reason rule 4 is swept: a stanza written next month
#      is covered the moment it is written. The two credential stanzas are the
#      one place the role appears without power, and they name neither power
#      nor user.
#
# Changing an export or widening a read here is a security review, not a
# convenience edit.

# Root default. Deliberately narrow: an object type not listed below inherits
# administrator and power read rather than becoming world readable by accident.
[]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# Search commands. Readable by any role that can run a search, since a user who
# cannot read the command definition cannot invoke it. This is the default for
# the object class; the two commands below override it individually, because
# they do not carry the same risk and must not inherit one permission between
# them.
[commands]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: polyswarmextract is readable by the user role.
#
# It is pure local computation. It runs the shared extraction engine over a
# field that is already in the result set the user is allowed to see, makes no
# network call, needs no credential, and spends no quota. There is nothing here
# a user could consume or exfiltrate that they did not already have, so gating
# it would cost capability and buy nothing.
[commands/polyswarmextract]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: polyswarmsearch is admin and power only. Deliberately excludes the
# user role.
#
# Every invocation spends the customer's PolySwarm API quota, one request per
# distinct hash, against a single shared account key. That makes it a
# consumable, shared resource, and three consequences follow:
#
#   1. A user running it over a wide search can exhaust the quota for every
#      other user of the app, including scheduled searches and correlation
#      searches that other people depend on.
#   2. Quota exhaustion and rate limiting persist after the search ends, so the
#      damage outlives the person who caused it.
#   3. The hashes queried leave the customer's estate. That is a data egress
#      decision, and it belongs to a role that is accountable for it.
#
# The required limit option bounds a single search. It does not bound how many
# searches a role can run, which is what this gate is for. The two controls are
# not substitutes.
#
# Widening this to the user role is a security review, not a convenience edit.
[commands/polyswarmsearch]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: polyswarmusage is admin and power only. Deliberately excludes the
# user role, and for a different reason from polyswarmsearch's.
#
# It is not the cost. Two requests to the account endpoints spend none of the
# scan or detonation allowance a customer buys, and the command throttles itself
# to one call a minute per search head on top of that. It is what the answer
# contains. The rows carry the account's plan class, its tenant, its entitled
# communities, and how much of each entitlement is left, which is the
# customer's commercial relationship with us rather than security telemetry. A
# role that cannot read the app's configuration should not learn the contract
# from a search command instead.
#
# The consequence is deliberate and matches the pattern [views/triage]
# established: the Overview dashboard stays readable by the user role, and a
# user pressing the refresh input there gets a permission error naming this
# command. That is the honest failure. The stored answer beside it is refused by
# [collections/ps_usage_cache], one decision in one place.
#
# Whether the user role should see quota is a real question for the usage
# surface to answer, and it is not answered by shipping a command. Widening this
# is a review, not a convenience edit.
[commands/polyswarmusage]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: polyswarmdetonate is admin and power only. Deliberately excludes
# the user role, and this is the narrowest gate in the file by intent even
# though its access line reads the same as the two above.
#
# Constraint C3 says commands that spend quota get a role gate, and this is the
# command that spends the most. An enrichment request is a database lookup. One
# invocation of this books a virtual machine, runs a browser inside it against
# a URL, and captures the result, which is the most expensive metered call in
# the product and the one with the smallest included allowance: the entitlement
# matrix gives an Enterprise plan 30 detonations a month, where the same plan's
# API request allowance is measured in thousands.
#
# Four consequences follow, and the last one is specific to this command:
#
#   1. A single search spends a detonation from a shared monthly allowance
#      measured in tens, so one user can exhaust a month for everyone.
#   2. The spend is not reversible and not throttled by anything except the
#      cap of one per invocation, and a saved search or an alert action can
#      dispatch this on a schedule.
#   3. The URL leaves the customer's estate AND IS BROWSED FROM OUR
#      INFRASTRUCTURE, which is a stronger statement than the egress
#      polyswarmsearch makes: the sandbox fetches the target, so the target's
#      operator sees the request. That is a decision an accountable role makes.
#   4. The command writes to the ps_sandbox_tasks collection, whose ACL below
#      matches this one, because a role that can detonate and cannot record the
#      task would spend the call and lose the receipt.
#
# Widening this to the user role is a security review, not a convenience edit,
# and it is a bigger review than widening polyswarmsearch would be.
[commands/polyswarmdetonate]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: polyswarmsync is admin and power only, and it is gated for a
# reason none of the four commands above share.
#
# It does not spend a fixed amount. One invocation runs a whole cycle of the
# polyswarm_intel_pull input, which is one API request per page up to that
# stanza's own max_artifacts_per_run, so a single typed command can spend what
# the schedule was going to spend over an interval, and can be typed again
# immediately. Constraint C11 bounds the size of that (the cap is the stanza's
# and cannot be raised from the search bar) but nothing bounds the frequency,
# because there is a person at the keyboard and the command exists precisely so
# that they can re-run it while adjusting a family list.
#
# So the accountable role is the one that already decides what the input may
# spend, which is why this line matches [inputs/input_intel_pull] below rather
# than [commands/polyswarmsearch] above. A role that cannot enable the
# scheduled pull should not be able to run its cycles by hand.
#
# The user role is excluded rather than merely unmentioned. Widening this is a
# security review: it is the one command in the app whose cost per invocation
# is set by somebody else's configuration.
[commands/polyswarmsync]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# Search assistant syntax for the commands above. Same audience.
[searchbnf]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# Event workflow actions, declared in default/workflow_actions.conf. The class
# default; each action below overrides it individually per rule 2, because the
# two actions do not carry the same risk.
[workflow-actions]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: the enrich action is admin and power only, matching
# [commands/polyswarmsearch] exactly.
#
# This is a different decision from the one taken for [views/triage], and the
# difference is the point. Triage stays visible to the user role because half of
# that page, the extraction panel, costs nothing and works for everyone; hiding
# the page to protect the metered panel would take the free one with it.
#
# This action has no free half. Its entire function is to dispatch a search that
# runs polyswarmsearch, which the user role cannot run, so a user offered this
# menu entry gets a new browser tab containing a permission error and nothing
# else. Withholding the entry costs that role no capability it had.
#
# The two stanzas move together. Widening polyswarmsearch to the user role
# without widening this one would leave the capability unreachable from an
# event, and widening this one alone would restore the failing menu entry.
[workflow-actions/polyswarm_enrich]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: the attribution enrich action is admin and power only, matching
# [commands/polyswarmsearch] exactly, for the same reason the action above
# does and with one more reason on top.
#
# It dispatches the same command the plain enrich action does, so the role that
# cannot run polyswarmsearch gets the same empty tab containing a permission
# error. And it spends twice as much when it does run, one verdict request plus
# one tag-link request, so if the two ever came apart this is the one that
# should be the narrower of them rather than the wider.
#
# All three stanzas move together. Widening polyswarmsearch to the user role
# without widening these would leave the capability unreachable from an event,
# and widening either action alone would restore a failing menu entry.
[workflow-actions/polyswarm_enrich_attribution]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: the portal link is readable by the user role.
#
# It is a hyperlink. It runs no command, reads no credential, makes no request
# from the search head, and spends no quota. The indicator it carries is one
# already in the result set the user is permitted to see. There is nothing here
# to gate.
[workflow-actions/polyswarm_portal]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# Alert actions, declared in default/alert_actions.conf. The class default; the
# one action below overrides it individually per rule 2.
[alert_actions]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# ROLE GATE: the detonate alert action is admin and power only, matching
# [commands/polyswarmdetonate] exactly, and the reasons that stanza gives all
# apply here with one more on top.
#
# It spends the most expensive metered call in the product, from a shared
# monthly allowance measured in tens, and the URL is browsed from our
# infrastructure so the target's operator sees the request. What is different
# from the command is the schedule: a search command spends once when somebody
# runs it, and an action attached to a correlation search spends every time
# that search fires, with nobody watching. The cap in
# bin/action_detonate.py bounds one firing; who may attach the action to a
# search is what this stanza bounds, and the two are not substitutes.
#
# The user role is excluded because it is excluded from the command. Widening
# either is a security review, not a convenience edit, and widening this one is
# the larger of the two.
[alert_actions/polyswarm_detonate]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# Modular inputs, declared in default/inputs.conf. The class default; the one
# input below overrides it individually per rule 2.
#
# Read for admin and power, write for admin, matching every other configuration
# object in this file. An operator diagnosing "why has this task not moved"
# should be able to see whether the poller is enabled and how often it runs
# without being trusted with the key that lets it run.
[inputs]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# ROLE GATE: the sandbox task poller is admin and power to read, admin to
# write, and the write half is the one that matters.
#
# The input spends API requests on a schedule, against the same shared account
# key every other metered path in this app uses, so who may change its interval
# and its per-run ceiling is the same question as who may spend quota. It is the
# narrower half of the pair with [commands/polyswarmdetonate]: a role that can
# buy a detonation cannot also decide how often the whole deployment polls.
#
# It is not withheld from power for reading, because reading an input stanza is
# how an operator answers whether the poller is running at all, which is design
# section 4.6's "why is nothing happening" question in its most literal form.
[inputs/input_task_poller]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# ROLE GATE: the intel pull input is admin and power to read, admin to write,
# and here the write half is the strongest gate on any input in this app.
#
# The task poller above spends nothing on its own behalf: with no detonations
# there are no rows and it makes no request. This input is the opposite. Its
# settings ARE its cost: families, the PolyScore floor and
# max_artifacts_per_run together decide how many metered requests the
# deployment makes every fifteen minutes, forever, with nobody watching. That
# makes editing this stanza the same act as authorising recurring spend, so it
# is admin.
#
# Reading stays open to power for the reason the poller's does, and it matters
# more here because the input ships DISABLED: "is the intel pull turned on"
# is the first question an operator asks when an index is empty, and answering
# it must not require admin.
[inputs/input_intel_pull]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# Dashboards and setup pages. The class default; each view below overrides it
# individually per rule 2, so that a view added later is a permission decision
# rather than an inherited one.
[views]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# The Overview dashboard, and the app's default view.
#
# Readable by the user role. Every panel that runs when the page opens reads
# either the verdict cache through the ps_verdict_cache_lookup definition, the
# stored entitlement answer through ps_usage_cache_lookup, or the app's own log
# lines out of _internal. None of those spends quota and none exposes anything
# a role cannot already reach through the collection ACLs above.
#
# One panel on the page can spend, the entitlement refresh, and it runs
# polyswarmusage. It is gated by a token no input defaults, so it cannot fire on
# load, and the command is withheld from the user role by
# [commands/polyswarmusage], so a user pressing the input gets a permission
# error naming the command. That is the same split [views/triage] makes below.
# See docs/dashboards.md.
[views/overview]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# The Triage dashboard, design section 4.8.
#
# Also readable by the user role, and deliberately so even though its main
# panel runs polyswarmsearch, which [commands/polyswarmsearch] withholds from
# that role. The two decisions are consistent: the command is where the quota
# gate belongs, and a user who cannot run it gets a permission error from the
# search with the command named in it. Hiding the page instead would produce a
# missing dashboard and no explanation, and it would also hide the extraction
# panel, which spends nothing and is available to every role.
[views/triage]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# The Sandbox Tasks dashboard, design section 4.3's tasks tray.
#
# Readable by the user role, and this one is the least arguable of the three:
# every panel on it reads the ps_sandbox_tasks collection through the
# ps_sandbox_tasks_lookup definition, no panel names a command at all, and
# opening it cannot spend a detonation or any other metered call.
#
# What a user gets is therefore decided one layer down, by
# [collections/ps_sandbox_tasks], which withholds the collection from that
# role: the page renders and its panels are refused by the collection, naming
# it. That is the same split [views/triage] and [views/overview] already make,
# and it is the honest failure. Note what widening the collection would show a
# user, since that is the decision this stanza is not making: the URLs other
# people detonated.
[views/sandbox]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# ROLE GATE: the setup page is admin and power only. Deliberately excludes the
# user role, and it is the only view in this app that does.
#
# Constraint C3 says an object that touches credentials gets a role gate, and
# this page is where the credential is entered. Three reasons, and the third is
# the one that is easy to miss:
#
#   1. It writes to storage/passwords. [storage/passwords] below grants that to
#      admin alone, so the write itself is gated whatever this line says.
#   2. It writes the app's own configuration, both the community setting in
#      polyswarm.conf and the is_configured flip on the app object.
#   3. It is a form asking for a credential. A page that asks for an API key,
#      shown to a role that cannot save one, teaches the wrong lesson about
#      where credentials go in this deployment. Withholding it is the honest
#      answer for a role that has no business handing us a key.
#
# WHY POWER AND NOT ADMIN ALONE, given that the credential write is admin only.
# The same reason [views/triage] stays visible to a role that cannot run the
# command behind it: the refusal belongs to the object being written, where it
# is one decision in one place, and a power user who opens this page gets a
# permission error from splunkd naming the endpoint it refused. That is a
# better failure than a missing page, and it does not widen anything, because
# [passwords] and [storage/passwords] are unchanged. Widening those is a
# security review; widening this line is not a substitute for it.
[views/setup]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# Navigation.
[nav]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# The nav itself. Same audience as the views it lists: a role that can read a
# view and not the nav reaches the app and sees no way through it.
[nav/default]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# Credential storage. This is the stanza the no-wildcard rule exists for: the
# API key lives here.
#
# WRITE IS ADMIN ONLY, unchanged and not up for discussion: saving, changing or
# deleting the key is an administrator action, which is the same audience the
# setup page's other writes have.
#
# READ NAMES polyswarm_analyst BESIDE THE TWO ADMINISTRATOR ROLES (ruled
# 2026-09-09, decision D27), and the power role is still deliberately absent.
# Every metered command reads the key from splunkd's storage/passwords endpoint
# with the searching user's own session key, so a role that can run the command
# and cannot read this object gets a working command and no credential. That
# was the live defect: a power-only user passed the command ACL and was refused
# the key.
#
# Naming the role here is only half of the fix, and the other half is not in
# this file. splunkd gates that endpoint with a capability as well as with this
# ACL, list_storage_passwords, which the built-in power role does not hold, so
# default/authorize.conf ships the polyswarm_analyst role to carry it. The two
# gates are independent: the capability without this ACL is refused by the
# object, and this ACL without the capability is refused by splunkd. They are
# lined up on the same role name on purpose, and moving one without the other
# puts the app back where it was.
#
# power is not added, for the reason it is not added to [app] either: it cannot
# use read access here without the capability, and the honest place to grant
# both is the shipped role. An administrator who prefers to add
# list_storage_passwords to power instead can, and then power needs naming here
# too; docs/install.md section 9 says so.
[passwords]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

[storage/passwords]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# App configuration, including the setup state that gates first use.
[app]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# The app's own configuration file, default/polyswarm.conf.
#
# Listed explicitly rather than left to the root stanza per rule 2. It holds no
# credential and never will, which is stated in the file itself and in
# README/polyswarm.conf.spec, so read is granted to power alongside admin: an
# operator diagnosing a scoping question should be able to see which community
# the app is configured for without being trusted with the key.
#
# Write stays with admin, matching every other configuration object here. The
# setup page's write to configs/conf-polyswarm is therefore an admin action,
# which is the same audience the credential write already has.
[polyswarm]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# Custom REST endpoints. These run with elevated capability, so they are
# admin only until an endpoint exists that has been reviewed for wider access.
[restmap]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = none

# KV store collections. Same audience as polyswarmsearch, and write is granted
# alongside read on purpose.
#
# The verdict cache is written by the search command as the invoking user, so a
# role that can run polyswarmsearch but cannot write the collection would get a
# working search with the cache permanently degraded: correct results, full
# quota spent on every hash, and only a log line to say why. Read without write
# is the worst of the three states, so the two are granted together.
#
# The user role is excluded because it is excluded from polyswarmsearch, and
# the cache holds nothing a user could not already see through that command.
[collections]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# The verdict cache itself. Declared in default/collections.conf.
[collections/ps_verdict_cache]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# The usage and entitlement cache. Declared in default/collections.conf.
#
# Same audience as [commands/polyswarmusage], which is what fills it, and read
# and write granted together for the same reason the verdict cache grants them
# together: a role that can run the command but cannot write the collection
# would get correct rows in the search bar and an Entitlement panel that never
# updates, with only a log line to say why.
#
# The user role is excluded because it is excluded from the command, and the
# collection holds exactly what the command returns. This is the one stanza to
# edit if the answer to "should a user see quota" turns out to be yes.
[collections/ps_usage_cache]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# The call throttle. Declared in default/collections.conf.
#
# Nothing reads this but the command, and no panel reads it at all. It is listed
# explicitly rather than left to the class default per rule 2, and it is worth
# noting what widening it would mean: write access to this collection is the
# ability to reset the throttle window, so it stays with the roles that are
# allowed to make the call in the first place.
[collections/ps_usage_throttle]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# The sandbox task table. Declared in default/collections.conf.
#
# Same audience as [commands/polyswarmdetonate], which is what fills it, and
# read and write granted together for a sharper version of the reason the two
# caches grant them together. On a cache, read without write costs quota and
# nothing else. Here it would let a role buy a detonation and fail to record
# it, and because the platform's own my-tasks listing hangs there is no second
# place to look the task up: the spend would be unrecoverable rather than
# merely unrecorded.
#
# The user role is excluded because it is excluded from the command. This is
# the stanza to edit if a read-only tray for the user role is ever wanted, and
# note what such a tray would show: the URLs other people detonated.
[collections/ps_sandbox_tasks]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# The cached providers and VMs list. Declared in default/collections.conf.
#
# Nothing reads this but the command, and no panel reads it at all. Listed
# explicitly rather than left to the class default per rule 2. Write access
# here is the ability to poison the list a detonation is validated against,
# which is the reason it stays with the roles that are allowed to detonate in
# the first place rather than being granted more widely because the content
# looks harmless.
[collections/ps_sandbox_providers]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# The community rule's whois cache. Declared in default/collections.conf.
#
# Same audience as [commands/polyswarmdetonate], which is what fills it, and
# read and write granted together for the reason every cache in this file
# grants them together: a role that can detonate and cannot write this
# collection would resolve the community correctly and pay for a whois call on
# every detonation, with only a log line to say why.
#
# Note what write access to this collection is: the ability to change the
# community list a detonation is scoped from, for everyone, for an hour. That
# is why it stays with the roles allowed to detonate rather than being granted
# more widely because the content looks like an account detail. The account
# identity it holds is the same answer [commands/polyswarmusage] returns, and
# that command withholds itself from the user role for the same reason.
[collections/ps_community_cache]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# The intel pull's checkpoint, declared in default/collections.conf.
#
# Admin and power for both halves, matching the caches above rather than the
# task collection. The reason is what a failure costs: the modular input runs
# as the app and writes this document at the end of a completed window, and a
# role that could read it but not write it would re-pull the same window on
# every run. Bounded duplication rather than lost data, which is the direction
# bin/intel_checkpoint.py chooses everywhere, but there is no reason to choose
# it here by accident.
#
# Power can write because | polyswarmsync runs for power and constructs the
# same store object. It does not advance the checkpoint, deliberately, but it
# does open the collection, and an ACL that made that a permission error would
# report a broken checkpoint on every preview.
[collections/ps_intel_pull_checkpoint]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ], write : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst ]
export = system

# Field extractions and parsing. default/props.conf carries one sourcetype,
# polyswarm:artifact, written by the intel pull input.
#
# READABLE BY THE USER ROLE, WHICH IS WIDER THAN THE INPUT THAT PRODUCES THE
# EVENTS, AND THAT IS CORRECT RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT. A props stanza is not a
# capability, it is how an event becomes searchable: without read access the
# JSON body indexes as one unparsed blob and every field in it disappears for
# that role. An analyst who can see the index has to be able to see the fields,
# and gating this to admin would ship a data source nobody can use. Who may
# CREATE the events is the input's ACL above, and that is where the spend
# decision lives.
#
# The CIM field aliases and the vendor_product eval are in the same stanza and
# inherit the same reading, for the same reason: a data model that resolves for
# admin and not for an analyst is worse than no data model.
[props]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# Event types. default/eventtypes.conf carries one, polyswarm_artifact, which
# exists so that default/tags.conf has something to hang the malware tag on.
#
# Same reading as [props] and for the same reason: an event type an analyst
# cannot read is a tag that does not resolve for them, and a data model that
# resolves per role is a data model nobody trusts.
[eventtypes]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# CIM tags. default/tags.conf applies malware to the event type above, and
# deliberately not attack; the file carries the argument.
#
# Same reading, same reason. This is the object a customer's Malware data model
# actually matches on.
[tags]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# Lookup definitions. The class default.
[transforms]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# The verdict cache lookup definition, declared in default/transforms.conf.
#
# Read is granted to the user role, and write only to admin.
#
# Reading the DEFINITION is not reading the data. The collection behind it
# carries its own ACL in [collections/ps_verdict_cache], which does not
# include the user role, so a user running | inputlookup against this
# definition is refused by the collection and not by the definition. That is
# the intended split: the lookup definition is public app furniture, and who
# may read the cache is one decision, made in one place, next to who may spend
# quota filling it. Widening cache access to the user role is an edit to the
# collections stanza, not to this one.
[transforms/ps_verdict_cache_lookup]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# The usage cache lookup definition, declared in default/transforms.conf.
#
# Read granted to the user role, write only to admin, exactly as for the verdict
# cache lookup and for the same reason: reading the DEFINITION is not reading
# the data. The collection behind it carries its own ACL in
# [collections/ps_usage_cache], which does not include the user role, so a user
# running | inputlookup against this definition is refused by the collection and
# not by the definition. Who may read entitlement is one decision, made in one
# place, next to who may fetch it.
[transforms/ps_usage_cache_lookup]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system

# The sandbox task lookup definition, declared in default/transforms.conf.
#
# Read granted to the user role, write only to admin, exactly as for the two
# lookup definitions above and for the same reason: reading the DEFINITION is
# not reading the data. The collection behind it carries its own ACL in
# [collections/ps_sandbox_tasks], which does not include the user role, so a
# user running | inputlookup against this definition is refused by the
# collection and not by the definition. Who may read the tasks is one decision,
# made in one place, next to who may create them.
[transforms/ps_sandbox_tasks_lookup]
access = read : [ admin, sc_admin, power, polyswarm_analyst, user ], write : [ admin, sc_admin ]
export = system
